1
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,860
Awkward silence.

2
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:09,150
Sheldon on his phone.

3
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:10,010
No touching.

4
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:13,000
Somebody's having date night.

5
00:00:13,690 --> 00:00:15,710
It's actually steamier than it looks.

6
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:18,460
Sheldon's looking up the phallic symbolism of root vegetables

7
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:20,750
in Renaissance paintings.

8
00:00:21,010 --> 00:00:22,510
Oh, no, I got bored with that.

9
00:00:22,510 --> 00:00:26,020
I'm just browsing cuticle scissors on Amazon.

10
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:27,940
How do you not tear off his clothes

11
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:30,510
and take him right here on this table?

12
00:00:31,370 --> 00:00:34,090
If you do that, I'll scream.

13
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:38,900
Sheldon, I have some exciting news to tell you.

14
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:40,050
That makes two of us.

15
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:41,660
My new cuticle scissors will be here

16
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:43,750
in one to two business days.

17
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:45,900
Come on, one.

18
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:48,520
I've been invited to consult on an experiment

19
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,850
at your university for a few months.

20
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:51,920
Isn't that great?

21
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:53,270
We could have lunch together.

22
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:55,030
We could carpool.

23
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:56,820
You know, riding with Leonard

24
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,950
has gotten a little tedious lately.

25
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:01,020
Th-The only car game he ever wants to play

26
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:03,040
is the Quiet Game.

27
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:04,220
And he's terrible at it.

28
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:06,110
I always win.

29
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:09,470
So, you're okay with this?

30
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:10,840
Well, why wouldn't I be?

31
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:12,780
Well, this project would have us working

32
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:14,640
in close proximity to one another.

33
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:16,340
And there's the vulgar adage

34
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:19,890
that one should not defecate where one eats.

35
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:22,880
My father used to say that all the time.

36
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:25,690
That and, um, "Who does one have to orally gratify

37
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:28,300
to get a drink around here?"

38
00:01:30,950 --> 00:01:32,100
But what does that have to do

39
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:34,310
with you working at the university?

40
00:01:34,310 --> 00:01:36,870
Sheldon, "Don't defecate where you eat,"

41
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:41,020
means don't have a romantic relationship in the workplace.

42
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:43,660
Really?

43
00:01:46,470 --> 00:01:48,040
Yes.

44
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:51,490
Yeah, I always took it literally.

45
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:53,960
That's why I have never once moved my bowels

46
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,770
in this or any restaurant.

47
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:00,670
I'm relieved that you don't have a problem

48
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:01,700
with us working together.

49
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:04,170
Not as relieved as I'm about to be.

50
00:02:05,470 --> 00:02:08,260
It's a brave new world, little lady.

51
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:36,080
Are you crazy?

52
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:39,000
You don't want your girlfriend at work with you.

53
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,020
Hell Clam.

54
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:43,380
Why not?

55
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:45,620
Hairy Fairy.

56
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:47,510
I think it could be romantic.

57
00:02:47,510 --> 00:02:49,670
You know, my parents met at his place of work.

58
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:52,530
Your father's a gynecologist.

59
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:53,780
I know.

60
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:56,890
What started as a pap smear turned into a date.

61
00:02:57,870 --> 00:02:59,200
Which turned into her working there,

62
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:01,440
which turned into marriage, which then turned into hatred,

63
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:03,970
which continues to this day.

64
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:07,260
Two-Eyed Cyclops.

65
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:09,260
Would you please tell him this isn't a good idea?

66
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:10,700
No, no, I think it'll be great.

67
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:12,660
Maybe next time he gets conjunctivitis at work,

68
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:15,950
she can hold his head and try to put the drops in his eyes.

69
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:19,340
Giant baby.

70
00:03:19,340 --> 00:03:21,230
That's a Raging Ogre.

71
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:23,460
Yeah, I know.

72
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:27,460
I'm just saying, I'd never want to work with Bernadette.

73
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:30,200
Can you imagine seeing someone all day long

74
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,340
and then you're supposed to hang out with them after work, too?

75
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:34,140
Hold on. We do that all the time.

76
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:36,170
You and I work together and play together.

77
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:39,530
I know, and it drove me into the arms of another woman.

78
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:43,870
Bipolar Bear.

79
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:46,680
Well, I appreciate your concern,

80
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:49,590
but I won't be seeing any more of Amy than I already do.

81
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:51,940
I assume we'll deduct any extra time we spend together

82
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:54,720
at work from our weekly quota.

83
00:03:56,550 --> 00:03:59,350
Please let me be there when you tell her that.

84
00:04:00,210 --> 00:04:02,170
Why? So you can see the look on Amy's face

85
00:04:02,170 --> 00:04:05,020
when she hears my top-notch idea?

86
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,710
Please, oh, please, just let me be there.

87
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:12,680
So, Amy, what are you gonna be working on at Caltech?

88
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,100
I'm leading a study to see

89
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:15,920
if deficiency of the monoamine oxidase enzyme

90
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,780
leads to paralyzing fear in monkeys.

91
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:21,810
If they're anything like humans, the answer's yes.

92
00:04:21,810 --> 00:04:24,680
Wait, you've-you've done this experiment on humans?

93
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:27,100
You mean like death row inmates with nothing to lose?

94
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,940
No, that would be unethical.

95
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:32,280
You know, not a lot of people know this,

96
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,290
but the monoamine oxidase enzyme

97
00:04:34,290 --> 00:04:36,910
was discovered by a woman, Mary Bernheim.

98
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:40,100
That's right.

99
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:43,430
My phone is just as smart as you guys.

100
00:04:45,010 --> 00:04:46,580
Amy, Bernadette, Penny.

101
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:48,300
Amy, Bernadette, Penny.

102
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:50,440
Amy, Bernadette, Penny.

103
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:52,990
He's never gonna stop doing that, is he?

104
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:53,960
I don't mind.

105
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:55,510
I'm hoping to put his love of repetition

106
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:57,780
to good use someday.

107
00:05:00,310 --> 00:05:01,710
Hi. Um...

108
00:05:01,710 --> 00:05:03,040
I've reconsidered.

109
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:04,760
Uh, you can't work where I work.

110
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:06,380
Enjoy the rest of your evening.

111
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:07,970
Sheldon.

112
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:09,660
I-I already signed the contract.

113
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:11,260
I cashed a check.

114
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:15,610
You are not going to come out of this looking good.

115
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:16,740
Sheldon, I don't understand.

116
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:18,330
I thought you said you were fine with it.

117
00:05:18,330 --> 00:05:19,260
Well, I was.

118
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:21,090
But that was before Howard explained to me

119
00:05:21,090 --> 00:05:22,640
how awful it would be if he had to work

120
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:25,030
with his significant other.

121
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:27,270
He said what?

122
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:29,120
Now, don't be insulted.

123
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:30,700
He just thinks too much of you

124
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:33,670
would be mind-numbingly tedious.

125
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:37,020
Excuse me, I need to have a chat with my husband.

126
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:38,650
Yeah, well, now, well... keep it short.

127
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:42,150
Fr-From what I gather, brevity is your friend.

128
00:05:43,270 --> 00:05:45,440
So, are we good here?

129
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,020
No, we're not good here.

130
00:05:47,020 --> 00:05:48,330
I am working on this project.

131
00:05:48,330 --> 00:05:51,220
Yeah, and you can't tell her what she can and cannot do.

132
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:52,700
Last week, you told Leonard he couldn't wear

133
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:55,230
his Wookiee jacket out in public.

134
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:56,650
That's different.

135
00:05:56,650 --> 00:05:57,280
I'm not going to the mall with

136
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:00,150
someone dressed dumb space bear.

137
00:06:02,110 --> 00:06:04,010
Sheldon, you don't have to worry about me bothering you.

138
00:06:04,010 --> 00:06:05,250
I'll be in a different building.

139
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:07,670
And we don't even have to have lunch together.

140
00:06:07,670 --> 00:06:09,280
- Really? - Yes.

141
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,360
Before all things, I'm a scientist.

142
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:13,390
I'm just there to do my work and, with a little luck,

143
00:06:13,390 --> 00:06:16,460
scare the living crap out of some monkeys.

144
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:22,450
You sure your mothlike personality won't be drawn

145
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:25,550
to this blazing fire that is myself?

146
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:31,410
More and more sure.

147
00:06:31,410 --> 00:06:33,440
Well, then, you have my permission.

148
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:34,540
I didn't ask for your permission.

149
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:36,480
Too late. No backsies.

150
00:06:38,410 --> 00:06:40,870
No, no, listen to me. Sheldon misunderstood.

151
00:06:40,870 --> 00:06:41,770
What I meant was,

152
00:06:41,770 --> 00:06:42,790
if we worked together,

153
00:06:42,790 --> 00:06:45,030
there'd be too much of me for you,

154
00:06:45,030 --> 00:06:47,320
not the other way around.

155
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:49,420
Howard, if you're going to lie to your wife,

156
00:06:49,420 --> 00:06:52,770
don't start the sentence with "Sheldon misunderstood."

157
00:06:53,310 --> 00:06:55,480
That's a dead giveaway.

158
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:56,780
Well?

159
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:57,590
Okay, fine.

160
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:58,940
I did say that, and I think it's true.

161
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,370
I think if we worked together and lived together,

162
00:07:01,370 --> 00:07:03,180
we'd get sick of each other.

163
00:07:03,180 --> 00:07:04,640
Yeah, but to be fair, he only said the part

164
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:05,940
about him getting sick of you.

165
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:08,630
For the love of God, why?!

166
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:14,710
What exactly do you think you'd get sick of?

167
00:07:14,710 --> 00:07:16,740
His only options here are to fake a heart attack

168
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:19,220
or have a real one.

169
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:21,940
It-it's nothing in particular. I...

170
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:24,930
Is it my voice? Am I too bossy?

171
00:07:25,650 --> 00:07:28,990
My arm is feeling numb.

172
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:32,100
Nailed it.

173
00:07:33,130 --> 00:07:36,490
That's the wrong arm for a heart attack, doofus.

174
00:07:37,790 --> 00:07:41,250
My point is, I'm sure there are things about me

175
00:07:41,250 --> 00:07:43,380
that would drive you crazy if you had to deal

176
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:45,050
with them all day long.

177
00:07:45,050 --> 00:07:47,360
Like looking me in the eye and lying to me?

178
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:48,880
Oh, well, come on, I just...

179
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:51,500
Where am I supposed to look when I lie to you?

180
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:54,650
Find somewhere else to sleep tonight.

181
00:07:54,650 --> 00:07:56,060
Oh, Bernie...

182
00:08:03,570 --> 00:08:05,120
If it makes you feel any better,

183
00:08:05,120 --> 00:08:07,440
Amy and I are fine.

184
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:12,140
I mean, really good.

185
00:08:14,170 --> 00:08:16,730
So, boys, how was the pajama party?

186
00:08:16,730 --> 00:08:20,550
You guys jump on the bed and sing into hair brushes?

187
00:08:21,170 --> 00:08:22,510
It wasn't a pajama party.

188
00:08:22,510 --> 00:08:25,090
It was just a couple of bros hanging out, giggling,

189
00:08:25,090 --> 00:08:28,010
eating cookie dough and watching Princess Bride.

190
00:08:29,950 --> 00:08:31,940
Please, stop talking.

191
00:08:33,230 --> 00:08:35,570
As you wish.

192
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:41,370
I mean, you know, Sheldon, none of this would've happened

193
00:08:41,370 --> 00:08:43,350
if I hadn't tried to help you.

194
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:45,430
Uh, it also wouldn't have happened

195
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:46,840
if, in the early universe,

196
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:50,450
hydrogen was a little more common or a little less common.

197
00:08:50,450 --> 00:08:52,960
This is fun. Your turn.

198
00:08:54,140 --> 00:08:55,370
- Gentlemen. - Hey, Amy.

199
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:56,880
Hello.

200
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:58,480
That was kind of icy.

201
00:08:58,480 --> 00:08:59,930
You two okay?

202
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:00,870
Oh, we're fine.

203
00:09:00,870 --> 00:09:02,260
As Howard advised,

204
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:04,890
she's merely respecting our professional boundaries.

205
00:09:04,890 --> 00:09:05,660
Smart.

206
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:06,970
Take relationship advice from a man

207
00:09:06,970 --> 00:09:09,890
who spent last night braiding Raj's hair.

208
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,670
Yeah, you make a good point.

209
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:17,070
It would appear I was worried for nothing.

210
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:19,000
Look at her, desperately wishing

211
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,760
she was over here at the cool table.

212
00:09:23,590 --> 00:09:24,860
Don't worry, little moth.

213
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:27,330
The flame will come to you.

214
00:09:31,090 --> 00:09:31,930
On the off chance that

215
00:09:31,930 --> 00:09:33,700
Bernadette doesn't call back and apologize,

216
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:36,310
how do you feel about Mexican food for dinner tonight?

217
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:38,490
I-I don't know. Sounds kind of heavy.

218
00:09:38,490 --> 00:09:40,770
That's 'cause you always fill up on chips.

219
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,100
And this is Dr. Gunderson from Stockholm.

220
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:45,740
Ah, Sweden.

221
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:47,440
Yeah, home of my favorite Muppet

222
00:09:47,440 --> 00:09:50,230
and, uh, second favorite meatball.

223
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:58,640
Okay, the Nordic reputation for lack of humor is well-founded.

224
00:09:59,510 --> 00:10:02,380
Boy, is his name Gunderson or No-Funderson?

225
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:05,700
Where are we going with this, Dr. Cooper?

226
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:07,670
Oh, please, I'm your boyfriend.

227
00:10:07,670 --> 00:10:09,770
You call me Sheldon.

228
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:12,920
That's right, I am in a boy-girl relationship

229
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:15,320
with this cute little lump of wool.

230
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:16,430
Sheldon...

231
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:18,500
It's a physical relationship, too.

232
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:22,330
Hand-holding, hugging... even on hot days.

233
00:10:25,410 --> 00:10:26,300
Okay, here's a new one.

234
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:27,730
Apparently now we kick each other

235
00:10:27,730 --> 00:10:30,630
in the shin under the table.

236
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:33,530
How do you like it when I do it to you?

237
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:37,930
Not so much, huh?

238
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:48,650
Test subject D7,

239
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:51,520
aka Betsy, fear response study.

240
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:52,740
Image number one:

241
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:55,560
Frenchman on bicycle carrying baguettes.

242
00:10:59,130 --> 00:11:01,270
No visible reaction.

243
00:11:01,270 --> 00:11:04,060
Image number two: sousaphone.

244
00:11:06,190 --> 00:11:07,650
Still no reaction.

245
00:11:07,650 --> 00:11:08,970
Okay.

246
00:11:08,970 --> 00:11:10,190
Let's kick things up a notch.

247
00:11:10,190 --> 00:11:11,090
Image number three:

248
00:11:11,090 --> 00:11:13,410
crocodile with a mouthful of monkeys.

249
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:19,810
Okay, now we're talking.

250
00:11:20,390 --> 00:11:22,040
Dr. Fowler?

251
00:11:22,590 --> 00:11:24,060
What do you want, Sheldon?

252
00:11:24,060 --> 00:11:26,130
Well, I'm done with work, so...

253
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:29,290
Oh, sorry, sorry, sorry.

254
00:11:30,230 --> 00:11:31,810
Sorry. Uh...

255
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:33,370
Better?

256
00:11:33,370 --> 00:11:34,550
Oh, baguettes.

257
00:11:34,550 --> 00:11:36,910
Yes, I like baguettes.

258
00:11:36,910 --> 00:11:38,980
What exactly are you doing?

259
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:41,340
Determining baseline fear levels in capuchin monkeys

260
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:43,990
by measuring their response to visual stimuli.

261
00:11:43,990 --> 00:11:45,920
So, goofing off.

262
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:48,440
As I was saying,

263
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:50,720
I'm done with work and Leonard's not.

264
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:53,360
So good news, you get to take me home.

265
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:54,390
Play your cards right,

266
00:11:54,390 --> 00:11:56,010
I'll let you drive me past the lot

267
00:11:56,010 --> 00:11:58,770
where the buses park at night.

268
00:11:59,220 --> 00:12:00,570
I can't leave now, Sheldon.

269
00:12:00,570 --> 00:12:01,700
I'm very busy.

270
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:03,310
Besides, why would I want to do you a favor

271
00:12:03,310 --> 00:12:05,590
after the way you treated me in the cafeteria?

272
00:12:05,590 --> 00:12:08,120
Image number four: boa constrictor.

273
00:12:12,490 --> 00:12:14,750
What on earth are you talking about?

274
00:12:14,750 --> 00:12:18,210
My behavior in the cafeteria was delightful.

275
00:12:18,210 --> 00:12:20,280
Maybe your friend Gunderson needs

276
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:24,100
to head over to IKEA and assemble a sense of humor.

277
00:12:25,600 --> 00:12:27,360
You embarrassed me in front of my colleagues

278
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:29,610
on my first day here.

279
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:30,680
Image number five:

280
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:32,690
kitten in a teacup.

281
00:12:38,010 --> 00:12:40,900
- Oh... embarrassed you? - You know what?

282
00:12:40,900 --> 00:12:43,490
I don't have time for this. Find another way home.

283
00:12:43,490 --> 00:12:46,180
I'm starting to get the sense that you're angry with me.

284
00:12:46,180 --> 00:12:47,800
Really? What tipped you off?

285
00:12:47,800 --> 00:12:48,600
Couple things, actually.

286
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:50,350
Your tone of voice, your body language...

287
00:12:50,350 --> 00:12:52,110
- Get out. - Well, now, that.

288
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:56,640
Image number six: woman giving birth.

289
00:13:05,590 --> 00:13:07,280
It's 9:00. Where you been?

290
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,360
Oh, I had to take the bus home.

291
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:13,290
Fell asleep and missed my stop.

292
00:13:13,290 --> 00:13:15,380
Oh, no.

293
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:19,270
Did you know that Los Angeles has a Little Sri Lanka?

294
00:13:20,670 --> 00:13:21,840
I did not.

295
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:24,110
Well, I do... now.

296
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:26,390
They're a lovely people.

297
00:13:26,390 --> 00:13:29,870
Although terrifying when you wake up face-to-face with them.

298
00:13:30,970 --> 00:13:32,260
If you're hungry, I brought home

299
00:13:32,260 --> 00:13:34,580
some mutton and coconut milk.

300
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:36,630
Why'd you get that? You hate lamb.

301
00:13:36,630 --> 00:13:38,760
I was asking for directions.

302
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:42,240
Apparently, there was a communication problem.

303
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:44,920
I thought Amy was gonna drive you home.

304
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:46,320
Yeah, I thought so, too,

305
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,730
but she's acting very strangely.

306
00:13:48,730 --> 00:13:52,150
I was discussing it with a Sri Lankan fellow on the bus,

307
00:13:52,150 --> 00:13:54,820
and he hypothesized that a tiger may have

308
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:57,400
recently run across her shadow.

309
00:13:59,010 --> 00:14:01,550
Although he may have just been trying to drum up business

310
00:14:01,550 --> 00:14:04,490
for his brother-in-law's witch doctor practice.

311
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:09,910
Okay, Sheldon, what happened between you and Amy?

312
00:14:09,910 --> 00:14:13,950
Well, can you believe she said I embarrassed her?

313
00:14:13,950 --> 00:14:15,330
Yeah.

314
00:14:16,430 --> 00:14:18,630
But you didn't even hear the details.

315
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:20,500
Sheldon...

316
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:24,660
I've known you a long time,

317
00:14:24,660 --> 00:14:26,010
and I'm going to tell you this

318
00:14:26,010 --> 00:14:29,650
with all the love I can possibly muster.

319
00:14:30,350 --> 00:14:31,970
Amy's right. You're wrong.

320
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:35,820
- But you don't even know... - Doesn't matter.

321
00:14:36,390 --> 00:14:38,840
- But, now, but in my defense... - Doesn't matter.

322
00:14:38,840 --> 00:14:40,580
You're not listening to my side of it.

323
00:14:40,580 --> 00:14:43,630
Okay, fine, Sheldon. What is your side?

324
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:44,970
Well...

325
00:14:44,970 --> 00:14:47,240
Nope, got to go with Amy on this one.

326
00:14:53,450 --> 00:14:54,920
Shrimp was spicy.

327
00:14:54,920 --> 00:14:56,060
You want a TUMS?

328
00:14:56,060 --> 00:14:57,420
You got the tropical fruit kind?

329
00:14:57,420 --> 00:14:58,380
No, if I buy those,

330
00:14:58,380 --> 00:15:00,250
I just go through them like candy.

331
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:01,270
Tell me about it.

332
00:15:01,270 --> 00:15:04,160
One time, I swear I pooped out a stick of chalk.

333
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:07,590
- I'll get it. - Thanks.

334
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:11,230
Hi.

335
00:15:11,230 --> 00:15:12,870
Oh, hi.

336
00:15:12,870 --> 00:15:14,880
I may have overreacted.

337
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:18,700
Yeah, well, I didn't handle it so great, either.

338
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:20,860
It's just sometimes I feel like you enjoy spending time

339
00:15:20,860 --> 00:15:22,750
with your friends more than with me.

340
00:15:22,750 --> 00:15:24,830
- That's not true. - It's not?

341
00:15:24,830 --> 00:15:26,460
You spend all day together at work,

342
00:15:26,460 --> 00:15:28,490
and then you all hang out at night playing games,

343
00:15:28,490 --> 00:15:30,110
going to the comic book store.

344
00:15:30,110 --> 00:15:32,830
Last week, you two got a couples massage.

345
00:15:35,690 --> 00:15:37,080
So, when you said you wouldn't want to spend

346
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:40,640
that much time with me, it really hurt my feelings.

347
00:15:42,430 --> 00:15:43,930
Yeah, no, I get that.

348
00:15:43,930 --> 00:15:45,310
I'm so sorry.

349
00:15:45,310 --> 00:15:47,650
Starting tomorrow, I am turning over a new leaf.

350
00:15:47,650 --> 00:15:51,140
Time with you is my number one priority.

351
00:15:53,190 --> 00:15:54,900
Why tomorrow?

352
00:15:56,160 --> 00:16:00,330
Well, we're real close to finishing off the new Batman game.

353
00:16:01,850 --> 00:16:03,720
It is awesome.

354
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,240
Uh, you should probably go after her.

355
00:16:09,240 --> 00:16:10,720
Should I go after you?

356
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:12,250
No!

357
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:17,010
Thanks for getting me in trouble.

358
00:16:20,450 --> 00:16:21,520
Amy.

359
00:16:22,010 --> 00:16:23,080
Amy.

360
00:16:23,780 --> 00:16:25,120
Amy.

361
00:16:27,790 --> 00:16:30,120
Sheldon, what are you doing here so late?

362
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:32,100
I couldn't sleep because I kept thinking

363
00:16:32,100 --> 00:16:34,830
about what happened earlier between us.

364
00:16:34,830 --> 00:16:38,070
Also, I had one heck of a bus nap.

365
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:40,640
Oh, speaking of which,

366
00:16:40,640 --> 00:16:43,500
do you want some mutton and coconut milk?

367
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:44,660
No.

368
00:16:44,660 --> 00:16:47,210
Well, I cannot give this stuff away.

369
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:50,340
What do you want?

370
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:54,300
Amy, this isn't easy to say.

371
00:16:55,310 --> 00:16:58,120
All relationships are difficult,

372
00:16:58,120 --> 00:17:00,560
but even more so when you're in one

373
00:17:00,560 --> 00:17:02,640
with a person who struggles

374
00:17:02,640 --> 00:17:05,150
with everyday social interactions.

375
00:17:05,150 --> 00:17:06,120
And frankly...

376
00:17:06,120 --> 00:17:07,880
who can strike some people

377
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:10,810
as being kind of a weirdo.

378
00:17:10,810 --> 00:17:12,540
Sheldon...

379
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:14,880
you're not a weirdo.

380
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:17,540
I wasn't speaking about me.

381
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:20,880
I mean, honestly,

382
00:17:20,880 --> 00:17:23,240
there's no telling what will set you off.

383
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:26,060
You know, introducing myself as your boyfriend.

384
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:28,620
Giving you the opportunity to drive me home.

385
00:17:28,620 --> 00:17:30,440
Breaking the ice with your colleagues

386
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:31,600
using ethnic humor,

387
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:33,700
the funniest kind of humor.

388
00:17:35,730 --> 00:17:36,900
What's your point?

389
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:39,590
My point is, we're a couple.

390
00:17:39,590 --> 00:17:40,900
And...

391
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:43,640
I like you for who you are.

392
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:45,450
Quirks and all.

393
00:17:47,310 --> 00:17:48,590
I like you, too.

394
00:17:48,590 --> 00:17:51,280
Well, I should hope so.

395
00:17:51,700 --> 00:17:52,980
I don't see anyone else banging on this door

396
00:17:52,980 --> 00:17:55,480
to put up with your nonsense.

397
00:17:59,930 --> 00:18:02,480
Not even a good-bye?

398
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:03,720
You see, that's the kind of thing

399
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:06,500
that makes people think you're weird.

400
00:18:06,910 --> 00:18:08,640
Poor kid.

401
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:10,480
She just doesn't see it.

402
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:17,430
I'm not apologizing to Howard.

403
00:18:17,430 --> 00:18:19,480
He can come beg me on his knees.

404
00:18:19,480 --> 00:18:20,360
Sheldon, too.

405
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:22,450
If I see him at work, I'm just going to ignore him.

406
00:18:22,450 --> 00:18:24,010
All right, don't worry. I talked to Leonard.

407
00:18:24,010 --> 00:18:27,300
He is gonna sit the guys down and set them straight.

408
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:29,200
Laser's warmed up.

409
00:18:29,830 --> 00:18:31,250
Pull!

